.. -*- mode: rst -*-

Current buidlbot status
=======================

This is the code for PyPy's buildbot, powering buildbot.pypy.org. It was
imported in April 2026 from https://foss.heptapod.net/pypy/buildbot. The
service is managed via ssh into the machine, and running the
``restart_buildmaster_when_not_running`` script. It uses a python2.7 venv.

Everything has been tested with builbot 0.8.8 on CPython2.7
Note you must pin "automat==20.2" "incremental==21.3.0"
Testing must use "pytest<4"


Patch the source
================

buildbot 0.8.8 is really old. There are some patches needed, see
patches.patch. These patch the *installed* buildbot package, not this
repository's own source, so they are not applied automatically by ``git
pull`` / ``hg pull`` and must be (re-)applied by hand.

The buildmaster is started with ``$PYPY_HOME/bin/buildbot``, so the install
that matters is ``$PYPY_HOME/site-packages`` -- see ``PATCH_ROOT`` in
``restart_buildmaster_when_not_running``, which refuses to restart until the
patches are in that copy. The paths inside patches.patch start with
``site-packages/``, so apply it from the directory above::

    $ patch -d $PYPY_HOME -p0 -F0 < <path-to-this-checkout>/patches.patch

If you are starting from a new db, you will need to also patch the installed
migrate and buildbot packages for newer sqlite3::

    $ cd $PYPY_HOME/site-packages
    $ patch migrate/changeset/databases/sqlite.py < <checkout>/patch-migrate.patch
    $ patch buildbot/db/migrate/versions/018_add_sourcestampset.py < <checkout>/patch-buildbot.patch

This only needs to be done once per install -- not before every restart --
but it must be redone whenever those packages are reinstalled or upgraded,
since that overwrites the patched files with pristine ones from PyPI.

How to hack the PyPy buildbot
==============================

If you want to run buildbot locally, you need to:

  1. create and populate a python2.7 venv with ``pip install -r
     requirements.txt``, then apply the patches described in "Patch the
     source" above to it.

  2. create a file ``master/slaveinfo.py`` which contains the password for the
     local slave you will run::

         passwords = {"localhost": "an_arbitrary_password" }

  3. ``cd master; make debug``

  4. ``cd slave; make debug``


How to run the PyPy buildbot
============================

``pypybuildbot.util.we_are_debugging`` returns ``False`` unless
``PYPYBUILDBOT_DEBUG`` is set, which ``make debug`` does, so a production
master needs no local change for it.

As above, make sure the install has the patches from "Patch the source"
applied -- this is easy to forget after reinstalling, and the buildmaster
will start "successfully" without them, only misbehaving later under load.

You still need to fill ``master/slaveinfo.py`` with the passwords of the
various slaves you want to use.

Then, to start the buildbot master: ``buildbot start <path-to pypy-buildbot/master>``


To restart the buildmaster
===========================

$ cd pypy-buildbot

$ hg pull -u

$ cd master

$ buildbot checkconfig

$ buildbot reconfig

OR

$ buildbot stop

$ buildbot start

OR

$ ./restart_buildmaster_when_not_running

Note: a plain restart does not reinstall anything, so patches already
applied remain in place -- you only need to revisit "Patch the source" if
those packages were reinstalled or upgraded.

